      "dom_id": "step-q139797",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "Given the QR decomposition $A=QR,$ find the least-squares solution to $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nQ=\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,R=\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ has orthonormal columns, ${Q}^{T}Q=I,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{T}Rx={R}^{T}\\,{Q}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${R}^{T}$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $({R}^{T})^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ Rx & ={Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting our matrices, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}}x & =\\overset{{Q}^{T}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]}} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]x & =[\\begin{aligned}33 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}-5{x}_{2}=33 \\\\ 2{x}_{2}=-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=-3.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}-5(-3) & =33 \\\\ 3{x}_{1} & =18 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the least-squares solution to $Ax=b$ is $[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
